14:05:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A documentary citing a study doesn't automatically mean the claim is solid — documentaries range from rigorous to pretty cherry-picked, so it's worth digging into a few things: **Questions worth asking about that study:** - Was it actual IQ tests, or something like the Wonderlic (used in NFL scouting)? These aren't the same thing, and the Wonderlic has been misreported in the media for years — people often forget it varies wildly by position (offensive linemen and QBs tend to score high, running backs lower, but this reflects the cognitive demands of each position, not overall intelligence). - What was the comparison group — other athletes at the same school, or some general population? "Other athletes" isn't a neutral baseline either, since sports self-select for different things (some recruit more academically-focused kids, e.g. rowing or tennis skew toward wealthier/private-school populations). - Was this NFL players, college players, or high schoolers? These are very different populations with different selection pressures (NFL players got there through years of survival-of-the-fittest recruiting, which isn't about brains at all). - Sample size and how "IQ" was measured — self-selected samples in documentaries are often small and non-random. **Even if such a study is legit:** A group average being slightly lower doesn't tell you anything about an individual player. It also doesn't establish *why* — could be socioeconomic background (football recruits disproportionately from lower-income areas with under-resourced schools), time demands, stereotype threat affecting test performance, or dozens of other factors that have nothing to do with playing football itself. Do you remember the name of the documentary or the study it cited? I'm happy to look at it more specifically — genuinely, if there's real data I'm not adding some kind of automatic assumption that it must be wrong. I just want to see what it actually claims versus what got simplified for TV.
