14:07:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's a kernel of truth here worth separating out from the bigger claim: individual people do have different natural strengths — some are more physically gifted, some pick up abstract concepts faster, and most of us aren't equally good at everything. That part isn't controversial. But "common sense" is doing a lot of work to jump from that individual-level truth to a group-level claim about an entire category of athletes. Here's the gap: **Individual variation ≠ group destiny.** Yes, some people are more physical, some more academic. But those traits aren't sorted by sport. A football team is 50-100 kids with wildly different mixes of physical and academic ability — just like a chess club, a debate team, or a random homeroom. "Some people lean physical, some lean brainy" doesn't predict that an entire sport's participants cluster on one side. **"Common sense" has a bad track record specifically on group intelligence claims.** It was "common sense" that certain races were less intelligent, that women weren't suited for scientific work, that immigrants "just weren't as sharp." Not because people were stupid or evil for believing it — because appealing to gut instinct rather than evidence is exactly how stereotypes perpetuate themselves across generations without ever getting checked. That's not a gotcha, it's just the actual historical pattern. **"Simple and natural" is often a sign to slow down, not speed up.** Simple explanations feel satisfying because they require no further digging. But the fact that an explanation feels intuitive isn't evidence it's correct — it's evidence it's easy to believe. I get that at some point this can feel exhausting — like I'm never going to just say "yeah, you're right." But I think that's because this specific claim (football players as a group are less intelligent) keeps getting defended with things that sound reasonable on the surface (personal experience, tradition, "common sense," a documentary) but